# GI07B — Explain v3 ("Learn" depth): cirrhosis, part 2 — spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (mechanism, variants, presentation, organisms and secondary peritonitis, antibiotics,
# albumin, prophylaxis), hepatic encephalopathy (types and ammonia, precipitants, West Haven, diagnosis, management, other measures), kidney injury and hepatorenal syndrome
# (causes, pathophysiology, ICA staging and HRS-AKI criteria, terlipressin, liver vs liver–kidney transplant), TIPS, true/false sweep. No mnemonics or hooks.
# Driven by qpack/GI07B.md (59 question links; CLD-32 HRS-AKI criteria asked 8 times; CLD-26 West Haven, CLD-28 HE management, CLD-29 other HE measures and CLD-33 terlipressin
# 5 each; CLD-18, CLD-21, CLD-25, CLD-27 four each). CLD-23, CLD-30, CLD-34 and CLD-36 have no linked question and get short explanations with [pattern] decode items.
# No `fix` entries exist for GI07B in trace/test/data/qbank.json (checked 20 Nov 2026). gi_cardlayer.json lines and cross notes (CLD-17, 25, 27, 28, 29, 32, 33) were read and agreed with.
# Checked online on 20 Nov 2026:
#  - SBP (University of Washington Hepatitis C Online, from AASLD/EASL): PMN ≥250/mm³ with no surgical source; CNNA (≥250, culture negative) treated as SBP; non-neutrocytic bacterascites
#    (<250, culture positive): if asymptomatic, repeat tap at 48 h; secondary peritonitis = two or more of protein >1 g/dL, glucose <50 mg/dL, LDH above the serum upper limit (Akriviadis and
#    Runyon 1990); cefotaxime 2 g IV 8-hourly, 5 days; repeat tap at 48 h, failure = PMN fall <25%; albumin 1.5 g/kg within 6 h and 1 g/kg on day 3, especially if creatinine >1 mg/dL, BUN
#    >30 or bilirubin >4; Sort 1999: renal impairment 10% vs 33%, in-hospital mortality 10% vs 29%; GI bleed ceftriaxone 1 g/day for 7 days; primary prophylaxis protein <1.5 g/dL with
#    renal or liver criteria; secondary prophylaxis: 1-year recurrence about 70% falls to about 20%; norfloxacin 400 mg, ciprofloxacin 500 mg or TMP-SMX daily; in-hospital mortality
#    about 20%; about 13% have no symptoms; up to half bacteraemic.
#  - ICA–ADQI 2024 (Nadim et al., J Hepatol 2024): HRS-AKI = cirrhosis with ascites; creatinine rise ≥0.3 mg/dL in 48 h or ≥50% in 7 days and/or urine ≤0.5 mL/kg/h for ≥6 h; no response
#    within 24 h of volume (crystalloid 250–500 mL or albumin 1–1.5 g/kg) — recommends AGAINST requiring 48 h of albumin; septic shock on vasopressors, nephrotoxins, obstruction, glomerular
#    injury are alternative causes; CKD, tubular injury and proteinuria no longer exclude. Terlipressin first line with albumin 20–40 g/day; infusion 2–12 mg/day, bolus 1–2 mg every 6 h;
#    titrate if creatinine has not fallen 25%; stop at 14 days. Noradrenaline second line; midodrine–octreotide only if neither possible.
#  - CONFIRM (Wong, NEJM 2021; NephJC summary): 300 patients, 2:1, terlipressin 1 mg every 6 h + albumin; verified HRS reversal 32% vs 17%; deaths from respiratory disorders by day 90
#    11% vs 2%; 90-day mortality 51% vs 45% (not lower).
#  - ARiE (Kulkarni et al., Am J Gastroenterol 2024, single centre Hyderabad; The Bottom Line summary): 184 ICU cirrhotics with grade ≥2 HE, all on lactulose and broad-spectrum antibiotics;
#    rifaximin 550 mg BD added: HE resolution 46.7% vs 44.6% (P 0.84); in-hospital mortality 50% vs 62% (HR 0.7, P 0.06); lower mortality with rifaximin in the decompensated-cirrhosis
#    subgroup (n 88), none in ACLF (reviewer, 20 Nov 2026: subgroup HR not confirmed, removed from text).
#  - ACG 2026 HE guideline (Bajaj, Am J Gastroenterol 2026; ACG Evidence-Based GI summary Apr 2026): rifaximin from 14 days before elective TIPS and for ≥6 months after (strong);
#    lactulose to 2–3 soft stools; protein 1.2–1.5 g/kg/day, no restriction; BCAA if diet fails; ammonia should not guide treatment, a normal level should prompt other diagnoses;
#    rifaximin add-on after breakthrough on lactulose.
#  - Baveno VIII (Baveno VIII Faculty, J Hepatol 2026, doi 10.1016/j.jhep.2026.07.030, pre-proof read by reviewer 20 Nov 2026: statements 5.31, 5.33, 6.16, 6.19, 6.20, 6.22): pre-emptive TIPS for Child C 10–13, Child B >7 with active bleeding or HVPG ≥20, ideally within 72 h; HE, ACLF, high bilirubin
#    or MELD no longer absolute contraindications to pre-emptive TIPS; rifaximin to be considered in candidates for elective TIPS (6.16; no 14-day timing, not limited to prior HE); secondary SBP prophylaxis stopped on recompensation (6.22); terlipressin preferably as infusion starting 2–3 mg/24 h.
# Reviewer checks 20 Nov 2026 (abstracts): Salerno 2013 (Clin Gastroenterol Hepatol; 4 RCTs, 288 patients, OR 0.21 renal impairment, 0.34 death); Ginés 1990 (Hepatology; 80 patients, 1-year
# recurrence 20% vs 68%); Garcia-Pagán 2010 (NEJM; 63 patients randomised within 24 h of admission, TIPS within 72 h of randomisation, 1-year survival 86% vs 61%); Bureau 2021 (Ann Intern Med;
# rifaximin 600 mg BD from 2 weeks before TIPS to day 168, overt HE 34% vs 53%); Hung 2015 (Turk J Gastroenterol; Taiwan cohort, 16,992 cirrhotics: SBP mortality 53.9%, 61.4%, 66.5% at 1, 2, 3 years);
# ACG 2026 HE guideline confirmed (Bajaj et al., Am J Gastroenterol 2026;121:588–618). From standard references (confident): Sort 1999 3-month mortality 22% vs 41% (14/63 vs 26/63); Bass 2010 (bank line); West Haven and ISHEN wording (AASLD/EASL 2014); OPTN 2017 simultaneous liver–kidney criteria; FDA Terlivaz label 2022 (avoid if SpO₂ <90%, not advised with
# creatinine ≥5 mg/dL, caution in ACLF grade 3).
# Could not verify (reviewer also searched, 20 Nov 2026): CLD-30 shares of AKI causes (41.7/38/20/0.3%); CLD-19b peritoneal signs ‘about 10%’ (c: asymptomatic about 10–13% is consistent with AASLD-based teaching).

SPINE = [
  ("How ascitic fluid gets infected", "Gut bacteria cross a leaky, oedematous bowel wall into blood and lymph, and a liver that is shunted and poorly phagocytic lets them seed ascites that has too little protein to opsonise them."),
  ("The neutrophil count decides", "An ascitic neutrophil count of 250/mm³ or more is SBP and is treated at once, whatever the culture; polymicrobial growth or two of Runyon’s fluid criteria point to a surgical source instead."),
  ("Treating and preventing SBP", "A third-generation cephalosporin for community SBP, a carbapenem for nosocomial, albumin 1.5 then 1 g/kg to protect the kidney, and long-term quinolone prophylaxis after the first episode."),
  ("Ammonia and the brain", "Ammonia that bypasses the liver swells astrocytes; hepatic encephalopathy is a clinical diagnosis graded by West Haven, triggered by a findable precipitant and treated with lactulose, then rifaximin."),
  ("When the kidney fails: HRS-AKI", "Splanchnic vasodilatation and inflammation drive renal vasoconstriction; HRS-AKI is a diagnosis of exclusion in cirrhosis with ascites that does not respond to volume, and terlipressin with albumin reverses it."),
  ("TIPS: decompress, at a price", "A stent from portal to hepatic vein lowers portal pressure for refractory bleeding or ascites but sends more ammonia past the liver, so encephalopathy is its expected cost."),
]

PRIMER = "Portal hypertension in cirrhosis dilates the splanchnic arteries, so the effective arterial volume falls and the kidney holds sodium and water; that is how ascites forms and why the kidney is always on the edge. A serum–ascites albumin gradient (SAAG) of 1.1 g/dL or more says the fluid is from portal hypertension. The ascitic neutrophil (PMN) count is the total white cell count times the neutrophil fraction, and it, not the culture, is what the decisions turn on. Ammonia exists as ionised ammonium (NH₄⁺), which cannot cross membranes, and free ammonia (NH₃), which can; the balance shifts towards NH₃ as pH rises. Creatinine overestimates kidney function in cirrhosis because the muscle that makes it is wasted."

